WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.960
Deep underwater, 4 km down, beyond&nbsp;
the reach of our star’s light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:12.780
lie these dark potato-shaped nodules. They&nbsp;
have been there for millions of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:19.380
slowly growing in size, and these oddly shaped&nbsp;
bulbs may hold the key to an electric future.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:28.680
We have known about these formations for over 150&nbsp;
years. In 1873, HMS Challenger even managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:34.260
collect samples from the mysterious abyss of the&nbsp;
midnight zone. Dropping kilometre-long hemp ropes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.840
with a collecting apparatus attached and by chance&nbsp;
pulling one of these nodules to the surface.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.400
Each of these lumps contain metals&nbsp;
that can help solves one of modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.520
day’s greatest supply chain issues. Battery&nbsp;
production. Containing manganese, cobalt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.960
nickel and copper , and they are&nbsp;
scattered all over the vast seafloor.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.980
It is estimated that a patch of&nbsp;
land just over the size of Ireland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:05.520
can yield over 54 million tons of metals,&nbsp;
collectively worth over 20 billion dollars.&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.600
Since their discovery, they have&nbsp;
served as no more than interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.520
seafloor features. Given that the precious&nbsp;
metal they contain can be mined on land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:17.940
there has been no incentive to&nbsp;
profit off mining the nodules.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.860
But, with growing supply chain woes, and&nbsp;
growing competition in the electric vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.900
market, dozens of companies now seek new&nbsp;
innovative ways to surface these nodules.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.780
We know frighteningly little&nbsp;
about the deep sea floor.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:43.020
As you descend past 1000 meters, all&nbsp;
light disappears, and thus, so does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:49.080
all photosynthesis. The only light that may be&nbsp;
visible is the faint twinkling of bioluminescence.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.900
Without sunlight, food becomes extremely&nbsp;
scarce, and animals rely on what is called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.900 --> 00:02:00.180
marine snow - the falling debris of&nbsp;
organic material from shallower waters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:05.340
Because of these extreme conditions, the&nbsp;
creatures in deep water have evolved to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:11.100
some of the most unique in the animal kingdom.&nbsp;
At 4000 meters, you may find a semi-translucent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:17.040
dumbo octopus scuttling along, or a gulper&nbsp;
eel with its massive expandable gullet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:24.360
or a viperfish with its terrifying dead eyes and&nbsp;
unbelievably sharp teeth. Deeper still, scavengers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:30.960
and detrivores scramble across the bottom, looking&nbsp;
for any morsel of food they can find. Animals like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.060
this have been found as deep as 11,000 meters&nbsp;
- at the bottom of the deepest parts of ocean.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:40.140
ROVs and some manned missions are&nbsp;
beginning to scratch the surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:45.960
exploring the depths of the sea, but it&nbsp;
is no easy feat. Only 4 crewed missions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.920
have ever been to the bottom of the&nbsp;
Mariana Trench. The deep sea being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:55.440
one of the last ecosystems on this Earth&nbsp;
that has been largely untouched by humans.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.780
This raises major concerns for the&nbsp;
impact any mining operation will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.820
have on the strange and wonderful life&nbsp;
residing among these metal-rich deposits.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:12.480
Early investigations of the seafloor in the&nbsp;
1960s and 70s revealed that the nodules are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:19.260
clustered in certain areas of the sea floor. Their&nbsp;
locations depend highly on environmental factors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:25.980
Needing high oxygenation levels and a source&nbsp;
of metal in the seawater, or seabed to grow.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:32.100
Similar to how the iron in steel reacts with&nbsp;
oxygen and water to create a layer of rust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.640
nodules are formed in layers of oxidized metal.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.900
For a nodule to form, a piece&nbsp;
of debris has to sink into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.360
oxygen-rich environment of the deep-sea floor.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:49.560
From above, free-flowing iron and manganese&nbsp;
ions dissolved in water react with the oxygen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.660
and form layers. These form nodules&nbsp;
at the surface of the sea floor. [2]

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:59.220
From below, the metals get “pushed up”&nbsp;
by concentration gradients through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.600
porous sediment, and once they reach the&nbsp;
oxygen-rich environments they react and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.940
start forming nodules 10 to 15 cm below&nbsp;
the surface. Depending on their depth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:11.700
different percentages of metal will be present.

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:18.300
While the majority of the metals in the nodules&nbsp;
are manganese and iron. Surface nodules are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.440
exposed to more cobalt while the deeper&nbsp;
nodules collect more lithium and nickel[2]

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:30.000
These layers form at astonishingly low rates&nbsp;
around 1 to 10 mm per million years. [2]

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.100
In the time it took for our ancestors to&nbsp;
spread out from Africa and dominate the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:38.820
these nodules only grew the width&nbsp;
of a human hair (.017- .18)mm. [3]

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:43.620
Scientists can predict where these&nbsp;
nodules would appear based on these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:46.500
environmental effects, and we have confirmed with

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:51.120
that they appear in high concentrations&nbsp;
in the pacific and indian oceans.&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.660
[4] Most of these zones fall&nbsp;
under international waters.

00:04:54.660 --> 00:05:01.080
So, the UN set up the International Seabed&nbsp;
Authority in 1994. Based out of Kingston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:07.260
Jamaica, to organize and control all&nbsp;
mineral-related activities related to the seabed.

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:13.020
Up to now, they have not granted any licenses&nbsp;
for mining operations but they have granted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:18.480
permission to a total of 19 exploratory&nbsp;
missions of these polymetallic nodules.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.340
Of which 16 are based in the&nbsp;
Clarion-Clipperton Zone (CCZ),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:29.400
an area just off the coast of Mexico. [5] Each&nbsp;
of these missions covers an area of around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:37.200
75,000 sq km, approximately&nbsp;
0.16% of the area of the CCZ [6]

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.520
Estimations for the potential yield of&nbsp;
a mine site this large are expected to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:45.000
be about 1.5 Million tons of wet nodules per year.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:51.420
A typical electric car battery has&nbsp;
35 kg of nickel, 20 kg of manganese,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:57.540
and 14 kg of cobalt. [7] A mine this large&nbsp;
would provide enough nickel for 400.000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.520
car batteries (16,000,000/35), enough manganese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.620
for 18 million (360,000,000/20 ), and enough&nbsp;
cobalt (1,500,000/15) 100,000 cars per year [1]

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:11.160
The value of these deposits is undeniable, but&nbsp;
we have never extracted solid minerals at scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:18.360
from the seafloor before. This is going to require&nbsp;
new technologies, and unsurprisingly many borrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:24.240
from the oil and gas industry who are all too&nbsp;
familiar with extracting value from the deep sea.

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:29.460
Companies first have to locate a region&nbsp;
where the density of these nodules is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:34.920
large enough to make extraction worthwhile.&nbsp;
Autonomous submarines are first sent down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:39.600
to access the area. Using pulses of&nbsp;
sound to scan and map the seafloor.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.300
The resolution of these scanners is too&nbsp;
low to directly detect individual nodules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:51.840
which are usually about 10 centimetres in size,&nbsp;
but they can estimate the density of the nodules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:58.260
on the sea floor, before sending another&nbsp;
expedition to collect physical samples. [8]

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:03.660
Now comes the difficult part. Extraction.&nbsp;
Many different solutions have been proposed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.760
and there are several problems to overcome.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:10.020
Some trials were conducted in the 1970s and 80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:17.280
and the knowledge gained in these trials all led&nbsp;
to the same proposals for a mining method. [9]

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.160
A self propelled rover would&nbsp;
be lowered to the seafloor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:25.440
attached to a surface ship through&nbsp;
a rigid riser and a flexible pipe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:31.140
These risers are technologies created by the&nbsp;
oil and gas industry and are well developed.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.880
Once the collecting rover touches down&nbsp;
on the seafloor it can move around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:38.400
remotely operated from above.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.400
The rover effectively acts&nbsp;
like a potato harvester,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:46.620
but instead of combing through the soil, most&nbsp;
rovers will use a water jet to dislodge the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:51.720
nodules and push them into the collector.
We now need a way to transport the material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:56.520
to the surface, and this is where most of&nbsp;
the technological issues are found. [10]

00:07:56.520 --> 00:08:00.300
Once the nodules are dislodged,&nbsp;
the collectors carry the sludge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:04.620
and the nodules through a separator.&nbsp;
The unwanted liquid gets expelled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:09.060
behind the collector causing a plume&nbsp;
of dirt to trail behind the rover.

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:15.720
The leftover slurry with debris and nodules are&nbsp;
now pumped up. Things get a little difficult here.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:20.940
Raising material over a distance this&nbsp;
large requires a great deal of energy.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:26.700
However, attaching some kind of rope&nbsp;
to raise material isn’t easily scaled.&nbsp;

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:32.640
Pumping liquid oil is vastly easier than&nbsp;
transporting solid materials. Currently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.860
two main mechanisms have been proposed to&nbsp;
bring the nodules up to the surface. [9]

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:42.720
One way is to pump compressed air into&nbsp;
the pipe, which creates the necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:47.640
pressure underneath the raw material&nbsp;
to lift it to the surface. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:53.940
this method has a very low energy efficiency,&nbsp;
at around 15%. The second option is to space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.940 --> 00:09:00.720
out submersible centrifugal pumps along the rigid&nbsp;
rise; this is currently the preferred method. [11]

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:04.740
Once at the surface the nodules&nbsp;
are separated from the leftover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:09.600
slurry. They are then dried out for&nbsp;
transport to the mainland. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:15.720
the unwanted slurry that is left over needs to&nbsp;
be piped down and expelled into the water column.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:21.420
This slurry is one of the primary environmental&nbsp;
concerns of deep sea mining operations. [12]

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:25.980
Releasing at the top of the water column,&nbsp;
where it can affect multiple levels of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.980 --> 00:09:31.800
ocean ecosystem is not an option. At the very&nbsp;
least, we need to pump the sediment down beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:37.140
the photic and disphotic zones, these are the&nbsp;
layers of the ocean that sunlight can reach.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:40.500
Pumping sun-blocking sediment above these layers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:44.520
would interfere with plankton and&nbsp;
other photosynthetic organisms.

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:49.680
An MIT study, published last year,&nbsp;
investigated the dynamics of this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:55.320
sedimentation for both midwater dispersal&nbsp;
and for sediment dispersed by the harvesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:59.940
rovers. They found their models lined&nbsp;
up closely with in field experiments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:04.740
where they released sediment in exactly the&nbsp;
same way off the coast of california. [13]

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:08.400
They found, thanks to rapid&nbsp;
dilution in the sea currents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:12.540
that the sedimentation rate on the&nbsp;
seafloor was drastically lower than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.540 --> 00:10:18.180
normal background sedimentation,and thus should&nbsp;
not interfere with filter feeders in the region.

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:24.180
There was also no evidence of toxic elements in&nbsp;
this extracted sediment. While it’s tempting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:28.620
compare this sediment to the toxic tailing&nbsp;
pools we see in traditional ore extraction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:34.740
these toxic waters are a byproduct of the ore&nbsp;
processing, this part of the process does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:39.240
happen at sea. This is simply surface&nbsp;
sediment being returned to the sea.

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:44.400
However, even if the plumes of waste&nbsp;
product have minimal impact, the mining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:48.780
operations have the potential to wipe out&nbsp;
species we haven’t even begun to study.

00:10:49.320 --> 00:10:52.560
Sea sponges use the nodules as anchor points. Deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:55.530
sea octopus use them to lay&nbsp;
and protect their eggs. [2]

00:10:55.530 --> 00:11:00.720
And with these nodules taking millions of years&nbsp;
to form, any surviving members of the species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:05.400
that depend on them would be left without&nbsp;
a home. Devastating an entire ecosystem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:11.520
which we have very little knowledge about, or its&nbsp;
impact on the rest of the water column ecosystem.

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:17.160
One of the few studies on the impact of these&nbsp;
operations was conducted in the late 80’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:22.200
by a German government-funded research&nbsp;
expedition off the coast of Peru. [14]

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:27.360
To assess the effects of nodule collection, they&nbsp;
carved out trenches and removed nodules from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:34.080
an area 2 nautical miles in diameter. The first&nbsp;
seabed disturbances were created in 1989 and after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:39.600
33 years, the seabed floor has not recovered,&nbsp;
The track can still be seen to this day.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:44.280
Filter feeders that depended on&nbsp;
suspended food had a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:50.520
decrease in population and affected areas showed&nbsp;
lower biodiversity than controlled areas [15]

00:11:50.520 --> 00:11:54.720
Studies in other sites like the&nbsp;
CCZ have also found a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:59.340
loss of life of up to 20% when&nbsp;
compared to controlled areas. [16]

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:04.320
However, the sad truth is that the alternative&nbsp;
is to mine these materials on land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:08.040
which itself comes with environmental impacts. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:12.120
vast majority of cobalt deposits lay&nbsp;
under the lush forests of the Congo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:16.680
To collect these metals roads need to be&nbsp;
built, and forests need to be cut down.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:22.680
Another study compared the total emissions of&nbsp;
both land and sea based extraction methods, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:30.000
concluded that deep-sea mining of these nodules&nbsp;
could reduce emissions by 80% for Nickol, 76% for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.780
copper, 29% for cobalt, and 22% for Manganese&nbsp;
[17] These reductions are primarily driven by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.780 --> 00:12:41.820
how these nodules are naturally occurring on&nbsp;
the surface of the seabed, making extraction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:47.940
relatively low energy, and by the fact that ships&nbsp;
are incredibly efficient at transporting loads.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:53.520
We also need to be incredibly conscious&nbsp;
of the impending climate crisis. Ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:57.960
temperatures are rising and with more and&nbsp;
more carbon being absorbed into the oceans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:04.440
they are slowly becoming acidic, killing off&nbsp;
entire coral reefs. We need to transition to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:09.180
renewable energy as soon as possible, and&nbsp;
these metals would accelerate this process.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:14.940
And there lies the dilemma that we, as&nbsp;
the human race, have to ponder. Where do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:19.800
we collect the materials necessary to make a&nbsp;
greener future? Is potentially destroying an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:26.340
unknown ecological system worth it to save the&nbsp;
ones we know for a fact are in decline. Is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:30.900
easier to find a new source of metals than&nbsp;
to fix the problems with land-based mining?

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:33.960
These are the questions we need to answer before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:38.700
exploiting this valuable resource in&nbsp;
the deep dark depths of our oceans.

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:45.120
Climate change is the greatest challenge facing&nbsp;
our planet and we need more talented engineers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:50.160
scientists working to solve this problem. That’s&nbsp;
why I talk about it so much on this channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:56.160
